      <h2>1. The Architecture of the Defence Pay Matrix</h2>
      <p>
        The compensation system of the Indian Armed Forces—encompassing the <strong>Indian Army</strong>, the <strong>Indian Navy</strong>, and the <strong>Indian Air Force</strong>—is governed by a distinct, specialized pay structure known as the <strong>Defence Pay Matrix</strong>. While civilian central government departments operate across Levels 1 through 18, the Defence Pay Matrix begins at <strong>Level 3</strong> (representing a Sepoy in the Army, Seaman in the Navy, and Aircraftsman in the Air Force) and culminates at <strong>Level 18</strong> (the Chief of Defence Staff and the three Service Chiefs).
      </p>
      <p>
        Prior to the 7th Central Pay Commission, military salaries relied on civilian Pay Bands coupled with Grade Pay and Military Service Pay (MSP). The 7th CPC harmonized military postings with civilian executive hierarchies while creating dedicated military intermediate tiers (such as <strong>Level 5A</strong>, <strong>Level 8A</strong>, and <strong>Level 10B</strong> for Captains) to accurately reflect the unique rank and promotion structure of combat commands.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Complete Defence Pay Matrix: Tri-Service Rank Equivalency Table</h2>
      <p>
        The table below outlines the tri-service rank equivalency across Army, Navy, and Air Force cadres, comparing 7th CPC baseline pay against projected 8th CPC scales at 2.25x (consensus model) and 2.86x (federation target):
      </p>

