      <h2>3. Military Service Pay (MSP): Statutory Purpose and Slabs</h2>
      <p>
        A defining feature distinguishing armed forces personnel from their civilian counterparts is <strong>Military Service Pay (MSP)</strong>. Introduced in the 6th Central Pay Commission and expanded under the 7th CPC, MSP compensates military personnel for:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Severe operational hazards, armed combat exposure, and life-threatening postings along hostile international borders and line-of-control (LoC) environments.</li>
        <li>Subjection to military law (Army Act, Navy Act, and Air Force Act) involving strict regimentation and forfeiture of fundamental civil unionization liberties.</li>
        <li>Frequent, disruptive unaccompanied transfers, family separation, and compulsory early superannuation (soldiers typically retire in their late 30s or early 40s).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="data-table">
